                <pb facs="0860" n="746" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg746"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">724.3415/1282: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Bolivia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Feely</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">La
                                Paz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-07-11T11:00:00-04:32">July 11, 1931—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 12:47 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>78. In response to my inquiry as to the status of the Argentine offer of good
                    offices looking toward an amicable settlement of the Ynsfran incident and the
                    resumption of relations, the Minister for Foreign Affairs last evening informed
                    me that informal conversations were in progress with Argentine Minister here,
                    and that he would inform me as soon as a decision is reached. Similar inquiries
                    were made by other Latin American missions. I also inquired when a reply to the
                    neutral Governments’ note of June 25th might be expected and he said it would
                    probably be forthcoming next week but it is my opinion that it will be further
                    delayed. [Paraphrase.] An Indian guide to a Paraguayan patrol near Fortin
                    Saavedra was <pb facs="0861" n="747" xml:id="pg747"/> shot July 1 by a Bolivian
                    sentry. No publicity, however, has been given in La Paz to the incident. [End
                    paraphrase.] The Bolivian Army is evidently being put in readiness to defend its
                    position, but the Chief of the General Staff yesterday informed me that he did
                    not contemplate serious incidents and that strict orders had been given to avoid
                    clashes. Paraguayan Minister left here for Lima, taking archives, on July 9th
                    with instructions to await developments there. Bolivian Minister Guachalla is in
                    Buenos Aires.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Feely</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
